caveats:
- **Search coverage is uneven.** Five angles produced no claim that survived verification:
  - angle 2: TUM CAMP, Navab and Fotouhi C-arm sets;
  - angle 3: Bern ISTB/ARTORG, Imperial, KCL, Leuven, Mannheim, Erlangen/CONRAD, and raw projections from Artis zeego, O-arm and Airo;
  - angle 7: labelled intraoperative spine fluoroscopy on the open repositories;
  - angle 8: grand-challenge and other challenge sites;
  - most of angle 6: MIDRC, BIMCV, MIMIC, Stanford AIMI and the RSNA challenges.

  The only same-patient pair counts come from LUMOS (280) and COVID-19-NY-SBU (27). These gaps are unsearched ground, not negative results. The one exception is Zheng Guoyan's current group (SJTU), which was reached through SheepSpineX.
- **Device and intensity data are mostly missing.** The detector type and stored-intensity format (Pixel Intensity Relationship) are unknown for every set except LUMOS. LUMOS has no PixelIntensityRelationship tag and mixes MONOCHROME1 and MONOCHROME2. The Arcadis Varic's image intensifier comes from vendor listings, not from the paper.
- **Ground-truth pose is not externally measured anywhere.** In the JHU sets it comes from registration consistency or from registration to projections of known geometry, not from fiducials or tracking. SheepSpineX does not document how its projection matrices were obtained.
- **Access routes are boilerplate.** Every request-only route rests on 'further inquiries' or 'reasonable request' wording, with no DUA or licence stated. Industry co-authors (Shanghai Maestro Surgical Robotics; Chongqing Bosscom) may restrict sharing.
- **Date-sensitive items (checked 2026-09-21).** The DVAP-Reg deposit at NBSDC could appear at any time; NBSDC was searched only by keyword. The JHU GitLab and Dataverse negatives cover public, published items only. Siewerdsen left JHU in 2022, and other co-authors' affiliations were not verified.
- **Inferences, not verified facts.** Three points in the findings are my inference from verified numbers:
  - LUMOS radiographs are vendor-processed display images;
  - the LUMOS 200-250 mm CT reconstruction field will truncate body soft tissue in renders;
  - the Huashan films could be labelled by registering them to their CTs.
- **One split vote.** The LUMOS CT-to-radiograph linkage claim passed 2-1. One verifier could not reach the ACM paper (403).
